=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Under IAS 7, in which section is the purchase of property, plant and equipment shown?
+ Investing activities || Spending on long-term assets.
- Operating activities || Not trading.
- Financing activities || Not finance.
- It is not shown || It is shown.
explain: Proceeds from selling PPE are also investing.
mistake: Placing it in operating.
tip: Long-term assets = investing.
src: Study text ch17 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Using the indirect method, how is depreciation treated?
+ Added back to profit before tax || Non-cash expense.
- Deducted from profit || It was already deducted.
- Shown in investing activities || Not a cash flow.
- Ignored || It must be added back.
explain: Depreciation reduced profit but used no cash.
mistake: Deducting depreciation.
tip: Non-cash expense = add back.
src: Study text ch17 - indirect method (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: An INCREASE in trade receivables during the year is treated how in the indirect method?
+ Deducted from profit || Cash not yet collected.
- Added to profit || Reversed.
- Shown in financing || Not finance.
- Ignored || Working capital matters.
explain: An increase in payables is added.
mistake: Adding receivable increases.
tip: Assets up = cash down.
src: Study text ch17 - working capital (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where are proceeds from issuing shares shown?
+ Financing activities || Raising capital.
- Investing activities || Not an asset purchase.
- Operating activities || Not trading.
- Not shown || They are shown.
explain: New loans and loan repayments are also financing.
mistake: Calling it investing.
tip: Raising money = financing.
src: Study text ch17 - financing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: The tax liability was $8,000 at the start of the year and $9,000 at the end. The tax charge in profit or loss was $10,000. How much tax ($) was paid?
answer: 9000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Tax paid = opening liability + charge - closing liability
step: 8,000 + 10,000 = 18,000.
step: 18,000 - 9,000 = $9,000.
check: 8000+10000-9000
explain: Tax paid appears in operating activities.
mistake: Using the P/L charge.
tip: Reconstruct the tax account.
src: Study text ch17 - tax paid (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why is a statement of cash flows useful?
+ It shows how the business generated and used cash, which helps assess liquidity || Cash is vital.
- It shows profit for the year || That is the P/L.
- It values the business || Not its purpose.
- It replaces the SOFP || Not a replacement.
explain: A profitable business can still fail through lack of cash.
mistake: Equating profit and cash.
tip: Cash keeps you alive.
src: Study text ch17 - purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where are dividends PAID by a company usually shown?
+ Financing activities || Payment to providers of finance.
- Investing activities || Not an asset.
- Never shown || Paid dividends are shown.
- In profit before tax || Not an expense.
explain: Proposed dividends never appear.
mistake: Including proposed dividends.
tip: Paid only.
src: Study text ch17 - dividends (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: An INCREASE in trade payables during the year is treated how in the indirect method?
+ Added to profit || Cash not yet paid out.
- Deducted from profit || Reversed.
- Shown in financing || Not finance.
- Ignored || It affects cash.
explain: Liabilities up = cash conserved.
mistake: Deducting payable increases.
tip: Liabilities up = cash up.
src: Study text ch17 - payables (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is a DRAWBACK of the statement of cash flows?
+ It is historic and does not show profitability || Limited view.
- It is easy to manipulate with accounting policies || Harder than profit.
- It ignores liquidity || It focuses on it.
- It is not required by IAS 7 || It is required.
explain: Timing near the year end can also distort it.
mistake: Thinking cash is easily manipulated by policies.
tip: Cash ≠ profit.
src: Study text ch17 - drawbacks (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How is a PROFIT on disposal of PPE treated in the indirect method?
+ Deducted from profit before tax || The cash proceeds are in investing.
- Added to profit || That is for a loss.
- Shown in financing || Not finance.
- Ignored || Must be adjusted.
explain: The full proceeds appear in investing activities.
mistake: Adding profits on disposal.
tip: Gain out; proceeds in investing.
src: Study text ch17 - disposals (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Profit before tax $50,000; depreciation $12,000; loss on disposal $1,000; finance costs $3,000; increase in inventories $4,000; increase in receivables $2,500; increase in payables $1,800.
q: What is cash generated from operations ($)?
answer: 61300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cash generated = PBT + non-cash items + finance costs ± working capital
step: 50,000 + 12,000 + 1,000 + 3,000 = 66,000.
step: 66,000 - 4,000 - 2,500 + 1,800 = $61,300.
check: 50000+12000+1000+3000-4000-2500+1800
explain: Interest and tax paid are deducted next.
mistake: Adding asset increases.
tip: Add back, then working capital.
src: Study text ch17 - indirect method (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: Interest payable was $500 at the start of the year and $300 at the end. The interest expense was $3,000. How much interest ($) was paid?
answer: 3200
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Interest paid = opening + expense - closing
step: 500 + 3,000 = 3,500.
step: 3,500 - 300 = $3,200.
check: 500+3000-300
explain: Interest paid is usually in operating activities.
mistake: Using the expense.
tip: Reconstruct the payable.
src: Study text ch17 - interest paid (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: PPE carrying amount: opening $120,000; closing $150,000. Depreciation $12,000. Disposals at carrying amount $5,000. Revaluation gain $10,000.
q: What was the cash spent ($) on PPE purchases?
answer: 37000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Purchases = closing - opening + depreciation + disposals - revaluation
step: 150,000 - 120,000 + 12,000 + 5,000 = 47,000.
step: 47,000 - 10,000 = $37,000.
check: 150000-120000+12000+5000-10000
explain: Revaluations increase the asset without cash.
mistake: Ignoring the revaluation.
tip: Rebuild the PPE account.
src: Study text ch17 - PPE purchases (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q014
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items are ADDED BACK to profit before tax in the indirect method?
+ Depreciation || Non-cash.
+ Increase in an allowance for receivables || Non-cash charge.
- Profit on disposal of PPE || Deducted.
- Increase in inventories || Deducted.
explain: Finance costs and losses on disposal are also added back.
mistake: Adding back gains.
tip: Non-cash charges back.
src: Study text ch17 - add-backs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each cash flow with its IAS 7 section.
pair: Proceeds from sale of machinery => Investing activities
pair: Repayment of a bank loan => Financing activities
pair: Income tax paid => Operating activities
pair: Dividends received => Investing activities
explain: Interest paid is usually operating.
mistake: Placing dividends received in financing.
tip: Trading, assets, finance.
src: Study text ch17 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: An asset with a carrying amount of $5,000 was sold at a loss of $1,000. What were the cash proceeds ($)?
answer: 4000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Proceeds = carrying amount - loss (or + profit)
step: 5,000 - 1,000.
step: = $4,000 (investing inflow).
check: 5000-1000
explain: The loss is added back in operating activities.
mistake: Using the carrying amount.
tip: CA ± gain/loss.
src: Study text ch17 - proceeds (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: Retained earnings were $60,000 at the start of the year and $80,000 at the end. Profit for the year was $35,000. What dividends ($) were paid, assuming no other movements?
answer: 15000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Dividends = opening RE + profit - closing RE
step: 60,000 + 35,000 = 95,000.
step: 95,000 - 80,000 = $15,000.
check: 60000+35000-80000
explain: Shown in financing activities.
mistake: Subtracting profit.
tip: Rebuild retained earnings.
src: Study text ch17 - dividends paid (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What is the difference between the direct and indirect methods?
+ Only the way operating cash flow is presented; both give the same net operating cash flow || Same answer.
- The direct method gives a higher cash figure || Same figure.
- Only the indirect method is allowed || Both are allowed.
- They classify investing flows differently || Investing is the same.
explain: The direct method shows gross receipts and payments.
mistake: Thinking the figures differ.
tip: Two routes, one answer.
src: Study text ch17 - methods (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: Share capital rose from $100,000 to $130,000 and share premium from $20,000 to $35,000 during the year (no bonus issue). How much cash ($) was raised from issuing shares?
answer: 45000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cash raised = increase in share capital + increase in share premium
step: 30,000 + 15,000.
step: = $45,000 (financing inflow).
check: (130000-100000)+(35000-20000)
explain: A bonus issue would not raise cash.
mistake: Counting only share capital.
tip: Capital + premium.
src: Study text ch17 - share issues (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where does a proposed final dividend (declared after the year end) appear in the statement of cash flows?
+ It does not appear || No cash has been paid.
- Financing activities || Not yet paid.
- Operating activities || Not paid.
- Investing activities || Not paid.
explain: Only dividends actually paid appear.
mistake: Including proposed dividends.
tip: No cash, no line.
src: Study text ch17 - proposed dividends (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: Loans were $40,000 at the start of the year and $25,000 at the end. What financing cash flow ($) arises? (Enter as a negative number for an outflow.)
answer: -15000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Loan flow = closing - opening
step: 25,000 - 40,000 = -15,000.
step: A repayment of $15,000 (outflow).
check: 25000-40000
explain: An increase would be an inflow.
mistake: Reporting an inflow.
tip: Debt down = cash out.
src: Study text ch17 - loans (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why are finance costs ADDED BACK in the indirect method?
+ Interest paid is shown separately, so the expense is added back to avoid double counting || Separate line.
- Because interest is never paid || It is paid.
- Because interest is an investing item || Usually operating.
- Because finance costs are income || They are expenses.
explain: Interest paid is then deducted in cash terms.
mistake: Leaving the expense in and deducting interest paid.
tip: Replace expense with cash.
src: Study text ch17 - finance costs (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Cash and cash equivalents were $3,000 at the start of the year. At the end, there was an overdraft of $2,000. What was the net change ($)? (Enter a negative number for a decrease.)
answer: -5000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Net change = closing - opening
step: -2,000 - 3,000.
step: = -5,000 (decrease).
check: -2000-3000
explain: Overdrafts repayable on demand may be part of cash equivalents.
mistake: Treating the overdraft as positive.
tip: Signs matter.
src: Study text ch17 - net change (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee ADDS an increase in inventories of $6,000 to profit. What is the effect on reported operating cash flow?
+ Overstated by $12,000 || Should be deducted.
- Overstated by $6,000 || The swing is double.
- Understated by $6,000 || Overstated.
- No effect || There is an effect.
formula: Error = 2 x the item
step: Correct treatment: -6,000; trainee: +6,000.
step: Difference = 12,000 overstated.
check: 6000*2
explain: Buying more inventory uses cash.
mistake: Adding asset increases.
tip: Inventory up = cash down.
src: Study text ch17 - working capital (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Put these steps of the indirect method in order.
item: Start with profit before tax
item: Add back non-cash expenses and finance costs; deduct non-cash gains
item: Adjust for changes in inventories, receivables and payables
item: Deduct interest paid and tax paid
explain: The result is net cash from operating activities.
mistake: Adjusting working capital before add-backs (order of presentation).
tip: Profit, non-cash, working capital, interest and tax.
src: Study text ch17 - indirect method (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: Credit sales were $200,000. Receivables were $15,000 at the start and $18,000 at the end. How much cash ($) was received from customers?
answer: 197000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Receipts = sales + opening receivables - closing receivables
step: 200,000 + 15,000 = 215,000.
step: 215,000 - 18,000 = $197,000.
check: 200000+15000-18000
explain: Used in the direct method.
mistake: Reversing opening and closing.
tip: Rebuild receivables.
src: Study text ch17 - direct method (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What are cash equivalents?
+ Short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known amounts of cash with insignificant risk of change in value || IAS 7 idea.
- Inventory || Not cash.
- Long-term investments || Not short-term.
- Trade receivables || Not cash equivalents.
explain: For example, short-term deposits.
mistake: Including receivables.
tip: Nearly cash.
src: Study text ch17 - cash equivalents (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Where is interest RECEIVED usually shown under IAS 7 in the FA syllabus?
+ Investing activities || Return on investments.
- Financing activities || That is for interest paid alternatives.
- Not shown || It is shown.
- Deducted in operating || Not deducted.
explain: Interest received is deducted from profit and shown in investing.
mistake: Leaving it in operating profit.
tip: Return on investment = investing.
src: Study text ch17 - interest received (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t4
status: review
q: A trainee shows the tax CHARGE in the P/L as the tax paid. Why is this wrong?
+ Tax paid = opening liability + charge - closing liability, which may differ from the charge || Cash vs accrual.
- The charge always equals tax paid || Rarely.
- Tax is never shown in cash flows || It is shown.
- Tax is a financing item || Operating.
explain: The tax liability changes over the year.
mistake: Using accrual figures.
tip: Reconstruct the account.
src: Study text ch17 - tax paid (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Cash generated from operations $61,300; interest paid $3,200; tax paid $9,000.
q: What is net cash from operating activities ($)?
answer: 49100
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Net = cash generated - interest paid - tax paid
step: 61,300 - 3,200 = 58,100.
step: 58,100 - 9,000 = $49,100.
check: 61300-3200-9000
explain: This subtotal is often the most important figure.
mistake: Forgetting tax.
tip: Generated, then interest and tax.
src: Study text ch17 - operating total (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q031
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Profit before tax $86,000. Depreciation $24,000. Profit on disposal $3,500. Finance costs $5,000. Investment income $1,200. Inventories decreased by $2,800; receivables increased by $6,100; payables decreased by $1,900.
q: What is cash generated from operations ($)?
answer: 105100
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: PBT + depreciation - gain + finance costs - investment income ± working capital
step: 86,000 + 24,000 - 3,500 + 5,000 - 1,200 = 110,300.
step: 110,300 + 2,800 - 6,100 - 1,900 = $105,100.
check: 86000+24000-3500+5000-1200+2800-6100-1900
explain: Inventory decreases release cash; payable decreases use cash.
mistake: Reversing the decrease adjustments.
tip: Think about cash direction.
src: Study text ch17 - indirect method (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: PPE at cost: opening $400,000; closing $470,000. During the year, assets costing $60,000 were sold. No revaluations.
q: What were PPE purchases?
+ $130,000 || 470,000 - 400,000 + 60,000.
- $70,000 || Ignores disposals.
- $10,000 || Wrong sign.
- $60,000 || Disposals only.
formula: Purchases = closing cost - opening cost + disposals at cost
step: 470,000 - 400,000 = 70,000.
step: 70,000 + 60,000 = $130,000.
check: 470000-400000+60000
explain: Using cost avoids the need for depreciation.
mistake: Forgetting disposals.
tip: At cost: add disposals back.
src: Study text ch17 - PPE at cost (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items are FINANCING activities?
+ Cash received from a new long-term loan || Finance.
+ Dividends paid to shareholders || Finance.
- Cash paid to buy a delivery van || Investing.
- Cash received from customers || Operating.
explain: Share issues and loan repayments are also financing.
mistake: Calling asset purchases financing.
tip: Owners and lenders = financing.
src: Study text ch17 - financing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Tax: opening current tax liability $14,500; P/L tax charge $18,200; closing current tax liability $16,300.
q: What tax was paid ($)?
answer: 16400
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Tax paid = opening + charge - closing
step: 14,500 + 18,200 = 32,700.
step: 32,700 - 16,300 = $16,400.
check: 14500+18200-16300
explain: Operating outflow.
mistake: Using the closing liability.
tip: T-account.
src: Study text ch17 - tax paid (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t4
status: review
q: A company revalued its land upwards by $50,000 during the year. A trainee includes $50,000 in PPE purchases. What is wrong?
+ A revaluation is not a cash flow; it must be removed when calculating purchases || Non-cash increase.
- Nothing is wrong || It is not cash.
- It should be shown in financing || Not cash.
- It should be added to operating cash flow || Not cash.
explain: Revaluations increase carrying amount without cash.
mistake: Treating revaluations as purchases.
tip: Strip non-cash increases.
src: Study text ch17 - revaluations (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: PPE carrying amounts: opening $280,000; closing $315,000. Depreciation $42,000. An asset with carrying amount $18,000 was sold for $21,000. No revaluations.
q: What was the total net cash outflow ($) from investing activities (purchases less disposal proceeds)?
answer: 74000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Net investing = purchases - proceeds
step: Purchases = 315,000 - 280,000 + 42,000 + 18,000 = 95,000.
step: Net outflow = 95,000 - 21,000 = $74,000.
check: (315000-280000+42000+18000)-21000
explain: The $3,000 profit is deducted in operating activities.
mistake: Using carrying amount as proceeds.
tip: Purchases less proceeds.
src: Study text ch17 - investing (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A company reports a large profit but net cash from operating activities is negative. What is the MOST likely explanation?
+ Large increases in inventories and receivables have absorbed cash || Working capital.
- Depreciation was very high || Added back.
- The company issued shares || Financing.
- Dividends were paid || Financing.
explain: Rapid growth often strains cash.
mistake: Assuming profit means cash.
tip: Working capital eats cash.
src: Study text ch17 - interpretation (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Retained earnings: opening $145,000; closing $172,000. Profit for the year $48,000. A transfer of $3,000 was made from the revaluation surplus to retained earnings.
q: What dividends ($) were paid?
answer: 24000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Dividends = opening + profit + transfers in - closing
step: 145,000 + 48,000 + 3,000 = 196,000.
step: 196,000 - 172,000 = $24,000.
check: 145000+48000+3000-172000
explain: Include all movements in retained earnings.
mistake: Ignoring the transfer.
tip: Account for every movement.
src: Study text ch17 - dividends (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why does a decrease in an allowance for receivables get DEDUCTED in the indirect method?
+ It is a non-cash credit that increased profit without any cash inflow || Reverse non-cash income.
- It represents cash paid out || No cash.
- It is a financing item || Not finance.
- It should be added back || Increases are added back.
explain: Non-cash gains are reversed out of profit.
mistake: Adding it back.
tip: Non-cash gain = deduct.
src: Study text ch17 - allowance movements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each item with its indirect method adjustment.
pair: Depreciation => Add back
pair: Profit on disposal => Deduct
pair: Increase in receivables => Deduct
pair: Increase in payables => Add
explain: Adjust for non-cash items and working capital changes.
mistake: Reversing signs.
tip: Cash direction decides.
src: Study text ch17 - adjustments (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Purchases (all on credit) $140,000. Payables: opening $16,000; closing $19,500. Wages paid $38,000. Other operating expenses paid $22,000.
q: What were total cash payments ($) to suppliers and employees and for other operating expenses (direct method)?
answer: 196500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Payments = (purchases + opening payables - closing payables) + wages + other expenses
step: Suppliers = 140,000 + 16,000 - 19,500 = 136,500.
step: Total = 136,500 + 38,000 + 22,000 = $196,500.
check: 140000+16000-19500+38000+22000
explain: The direct method lists gross payments.
mistake: Using purchases as payments.
tip: Rebuild payables.
src: Study text ch17 - direct method (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee shows a bonus issue of shares as a financing inflow. What is wrong?
+ A bonus issue raises no cash, so it does not appear in the statement of cash flows || Non-cash.
- Nothing is wrong || No cash.
- It should be investing || No cash.
- It should be operating || No cash.
explain: Exclude bonus issues when working out share issue cash.
mistake: Including bonus issues.
tip: No cash, no line.
src: Study text ch17 - bonus issues (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Share capital: opening $200,000; closing $260,000. Share premium: opening $40,000; closing $50,000. During the year there was a bonus issue of $20,000 funded from share premium.
q: What cash ($) was raised from share issues?
answer: 70000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Cash = total increase in capital and premium (bonus issue nets to nil)
step: Share capital +60,000 (20,000 of it bonus); premium +10,000 after a 20,000 bonus deduction.
step: Cash = 40,000 + 30,000 = $70,000.
check: (260000-200000)+(50000-40000)
explain: The bonus issue moves amounts within equity, so the total increase is cash.
mistake: Subtracting the bonus issue twice.
tip: Total equity increase from shares = cash.
src: Study text ch17 - share issues (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Why is a cash flow statement harder to manipulate than profit?
+ Cash flows are factual movements, less affected by accounting estimates and policies || Objective.
- It is audited more strictly || Not the reason.
- It contains no figures || It does.
- It is prepared by the bank || Not true.
explain: Timing of transactions near year end can still distort it.
mistake: Thinking it cannot be distorted at all.
tip: Cash is cash.
src: Study text ch17 - benefits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Net cash from operating activities $49,100. Net cash used in investing activities $74,000. Net cash from financing activities $20,000. Opening cash $8,000.
q: What is closing cash ($)?
answer: 3100
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Closing = opening + operating - investing + financing
step: 49,100 - 74,000 + 20,000 = -4,900.
step: 8,000 - 4,900 = $3,100.
check: 8000+49100-74000+20000
explain: The statement reconciles to the SOFP cash figure.
mistake: Adding investing outflows.
tip: Net change plus opening.
src: Study text ch17 - reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t4
status: review
q: A company's PPE account shows a large increase, but the investing section shows small purchases. What is the MOST likely explanation?
+ Assets were revalued upwards or acquired without cash (eg under finance arrangements) || Non-cash increases.
- Depreciation was very high || Reduces PPE.
- Disposals were large || Reduces PPE.
- The statement is always wrong || Not a valid conclusion.
explain: Non-cash increases do not appear in cash flows.
mistake: Expecting all PPE increases to be cash.
tip: Look for non-cash movements.
src: Study text ch17 - interpretation (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Interest receivable: opening $400; closing $650. Investment income in profit or loss $2,100.
q: How much interest ($) was received in cash?
answer: 1850
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Received = opening receivable + income - closing receivable
step: 400 + 2,100 = 2,500.
step: 2,500 - 650 = $1,850.
check: 400+2100-650
explain: Shown in investing activities.
mistake: Using the income figure.
tip: Rebuild the receivable.
src: Study text ch17 - interest received (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which item would be DEDUCTED from profit before tax in the indirect method?
+ Investment income included in profit || Shown in investing.
- Depreciation || Added back.
- Loss on disposal || Added back.
- Finance costs || Added back.
explain: The cash received is shown in investing activities.
mistake: Leaving investment income in operating.
tip: Investing income out.
src: Study text ch17 - deductions (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which classification is correct under IAS 7?
+ Operating: cash from trading, tax, interest paid; Investing: PPE bought and sold; Financing: shares, loans, dividends paid || Core classification.
- Operating: share issues || Financing.
- Investing: dividends paid || Financing.
- Financing: purchase of PPE || Investing.
explain: Interest and dividends received are investing.
mistake: Mixing sections.
tip: Trading, assets, funding.
src: Study text ch17 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: In the indirect method, how are working capital changes treated?
+ Deduct increases in inventories and receivables; add increases in payables (reverse for decreases) || Core rule.
- Add increases in inventories || Deduct.
- Deduct increases in payables || Add.
- Ignore them || They matter.
explain: Asset increases use cash; liability increases save cash.
mistake: Reversing signs.
tip: Assets up = cash down.
src: Study text ch17 - working capital (concept basis; original question)
